### Ran Playwright code
```js
await page.evaluate('() => ((() => { window.__lume.start(); window.__lume.begin(); return {state: __lume.state, level: __lume.level}; })())');
```

Collected 0 but seeds moved (they fall). Also note light dropped from 100 to 62 in ~1.5 seconds! That's way too fast. 2.8/s should lose 4 units in 1.5s. Something wrong: multiple update loops running? Perhaps `requestAnimationFrame` registered multiple times because reload + start? No, reload resets. But maybe time accumulates and drain uses dt incorrectly? dt clamped to 0.034. Hmm 38 light lost in ~1.5s means drain ~25/s. Possibly `update(dt)` called twice per frame? We call update then render then requestAnimationFrame once. Fine.

Wait, maybe many seconds passed between click retry and the eval due to command latency (each playwright-cli call spins up a daemon/connection taking ~5-10s). The timestamps: click retry, then eval (maybe took 10s), then mousemove, sleep 0.7, eval. The elapsed real time could be 15-20s → light 62 plausible (roughly 38/2.8 = 13.5s). Yes each CLI call is slow. So drain is fine.

Collected 0 because Lumi maybe never reached the seed due to my coordinate conversion error. Seed at world (454.9, 336.6) → page (78.2+399.2, 94.5+295.5) = (477, 390), not (608,365). I mistakenly used lumi? I moved to 608,365 which is world ((608-78)/0.8778=604, (365-94.5)/0.8778=308). Lumi follows pointer to (604,308) — should collect seed at (455,337)? distance 150 → no.

Let me compute correctly and move. Also seed fell meanwhile. Better test differently: use a script in one eval to move pointer? Can't set internal pointer. But we can move mouse via Playwright code inside eval? No, page eval can't control mouse. However I can dispatch synthetic pointermove events on canvas with clientX/clientY! That will set pointer target. Then wait inside the eval (setTimeout promise) and read after e.g. 1.5s in the same eval, avoiding CLI latency. Let's do that:

```js
async () => {
  const rect = document.getElementById('stage').getBoundingClientRect();
  const seed = __lume.seedPositions()[1];
  const sx = rect.x + (rect.width/1280)*seed.x;
  const sy = rect.y + (rect.height/720)*seed.y;
  const canvas = document.getElementById('game');
  canvas.dispatchEvent(new PointerEvent('pointermove', {clientX:sx, clientY:sy, bubbles:true, pointerType:'mouse'}));
  await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,1200));
  return {collected:__lume.collected, lumi:__lume.lumiPosition()};
}
```
But pointer event dispatch on canvas: listener attached to canvas, ours sets pointer coords. Good.

Also Lumi target derived from pointer.active=true. Should work.

