content.text:
Mobile portrait title is broken as expected: card is clipped by stage overflow hidden; only part visible, CTA cut off. Unacceptable.

Need a better mobile portrait experience. Options:

1. Make the overlay escape the stage in portrait? If the title card overflowed outside stage (stage overflow visible) it might overlay page, but canvas stage clipping is on `.stage` with border-radius/overflow hidden. We could move overlays to be fixed/fullscreen on mobile portrait for title/menus, while canvas stays small. That's actually a decent approach:

On portrait phones, `.overlay` position: fixed; inset: 0; z-index: 50; padding with safe areas; card max-width 92vw; card scrolls if needed. Background dark enough to focus. The game canvas remains small behind. HUD would be above overlay (z-index 7) though; need to hide HUD when a fullscreen overlay open on mobile or give overlay higher z-index. Overlay z-index 6 vs HUD 7 → HUD would appear above fixed overlay near top? HUD is inside stage (position absolute relative to stage), so it's at stage top, not page top; overlay fixed covers page but HUD would show above overlay at stage. Slightly odd. Could set overlay z-index 20 in portrait and HUD z-index ... HUD is inside stage's stacking context (stage has isolation:isolate), so its z-index only applies within stage; overlay fixed also inside stage's stacking context! Since stage has isolation and overflow hidden, a fixed child gets clipped by overflow hidden? position:fixed child is clipped by ancestor with overflow hidden only if there is a transform/filter/contain? Fixed positioning escapes overflow but `isolation` creates stacking context not containing block. Actually fixed elements are positioned relative to viewport and not clipped by ancestor overflow unless ancestor has transform/filter/backdrop-filter/contain. `.stage` has `isolation:isolate` (creates stacking context but doesn't clip fixed; overflow hidden does clip fixed? fixed elements ignore ancestor overflow unless transformed). Not reliable.

2. Better approach for portrait: don't use the 16:9 stage. Make the stage fill most of the viewport with a **portrait layout**, and adapt the game world:

- Canvas can stay 16:9 logical world, but we can render in a "cover"-ish letterbox: stage is tall, canvas draws the 16:9 world scaled to width, vertically centered; the top/bottom extra space is filled with a page gradient and used by HUD/title. But overlays are inside stage and can use the full tall stage, so the title card fits! The gameplay world would be a 16:9 band in the middle of a tall stage, with dead space above/below. Not ideal but overlays look good; gameplay small.

Could fill dead space with a subtle blurred version of the scene (canvas background) to look intentional. Complexity.

3. Simplest polished approach: for portrait phones, show a **dedicated portrait welcome/game screen using CSS layout**, and ask to rotate for gameplay. I.e., in portrait the stage is hidden and replaced by a "rotate device" screen with the logo and a cute illustration. But the user may never rotate; the site looks intentional though. Mobile landscape works.

This is what many landscape-only games do. It's honest: "Este jardim foi feito para a tela deitada." with a button "Jogar assim mesmo" that reveals the compact landscape stage (and its title overlay now broken?). We'd need the title card to be usable at 358x201 → no.

4. Make stage height taller for portrait (e.g., aspect-ratio 9/16 or 3/4) and make the game world render appropriately: draw with `scale = max(w/W, h/H)`? Using cover crops 16:9 into portrait, cropping a LOT horizontally (like 60% of the field). Bad.

5. Adapt game to portrait: world coordinate space fixed 1280x720 but render "contain" with extra space; let the game background extend with decorative sky. The 16:9 area occupies full width and 201px height. Hmm.

6. **Use CSS `zoom`/scale for portrait?** Rotate the stage 90 degrees inside a portrait viewport, so gameplay uses the full height as width? Classic trick: `.stage { transform: rotate(90deg); width: 100svh; height: 100vw; }` with a wrapper to occupy the portrait screen. Then the game appears sideways — requires user to rotate phone physically to view it, which is effectively the same as asking to rotate but automatic and disorienting.

7. Design the title overlay to fit even in 201px: use a compact mobile variant. The card could be absolutely positioned over the stage with minimal content: logo 32px + one-line tagline hidden + small CTA + hearts. But the stage is 358x201; a card with 40px padding won't fit. Could make the title overlay **cover the full page** via a portal: move overlay out of stage into body on mobile? We can position overlay `position: fixed` and set stage `overflow: visible` for overlay... Or simpler: on mobile portrait, hide the in-stage overlay for title and show a **separate full-screen title layer** in the page flow (HTML duplicate or reuse via JS moving the overlay element to body).

Actually we can restructure the DOM: make `.overlay` a sibling of stage inside `.stage-wrap`, positioned absolute over the stage normally. On mobile portrait, use `position: fixed; inset: 0` with high z-index; since overlay is not inside stage, no clipping issue. This is clean!

Let's do that:
- Move `<div class="overlay" id="overlay"></div>` and combo outside stage? Combo should stay in stage (top center). Overlay currently inside stage. Move overlay to be a sibling after stage within `.stage-wrap` with `position:absolute; inset:0` matching stage? But .stage-wrap is flex column with stage + rotate hint + underbar; absolute overlay inset 0 would cover full wrap including underbar, not just stage. Could wrap stage in a `.stage-shell` (position relative) that contains canvas/hud/overlay and has the stage dimensions... essentially the stage itself. Hmm.

Alternative: keep overlay in stage for desktop; on mobile portrait, use CSS `position: fixed` on `.overlay` and rely on it escaping stage clipping. Need to verify whether overflow:hidden clips fixed descendants. Per CSS spec, fixed-position elements are not clipped by ancestor overflow unless the ancestor establishes a containing block via transform/perspective/filter/will-change/contain. `isolation:isolate` establishes stacking context but not containing block for fixed. `backdrop-filter` on overlay itself not ancestor. `.stage` has overflow hidden + isolation isolate + border-radius. Fixed child should escape clipping (I believe). But the stacking context of stage (isolation) traps z-index relative to siblings: overlay fixed inside stage with z-index 6 would be painted within stage's stacking context, and stage is a sibling of topbar; if stage comes after topbar, overlay paints above topbar within page stacking. Fine.

Let me test quickly: set CSS mobile portrait:
```css
@media (orientation: portrait) and (max-width: 720px) {
  .overlay { position: fixed; inset: 0; padding: 20px 16px calc(20px + env(safe-area-inset-bottom)); border-radius: 0; }
  .overlay .card { width: min(480px, 100%); max-height: 86svh; overflow: auto; }
  .overlay[data-screen='title'] { ... center + panel ... }
}
```
If fixed escapes clipping, the title card becomes full-screen modal, usable. The stage remains 358x201 behind, showing the game scene (small). While playing, overlay hidden and game is small but functional. Add rotate hint for a better experience. That's acceptable! Users in portrait can play small or rotate.

But HUD at 358px width: light chip 100px + seed chip + score + buttons might overflow. At <=420px we reduce paddings; seed chip ~65, score ~50, 2 buttons ~34*2+gaps → total ~300 + gaps ~20 = 320; plus left chip 100 = 420 > 358. Will wrap/overflow. Need mobile HUD compaction: on very small widths, hide score chip? Or shrink. Let's test.

Better mobile portrait game area could be bigger: use width 100% and height auto 16:9 = 201px, HUD ~48px tall covers a quarter. Small but okay.

Alternatively on portrait, we can let the stage size be `width:100%; aspect-ratio: 4/3;` and render the 16:9 world with `contain` → world visible 358x201 centered in 268px tall stage with dark bands 33px top/bottom. More vertical room, HUD still overlays top band. Character/seeds still 201px tall. Not much gain.

I'll go with fixed overlay for portrait plus rotate hint. It's pragmatic.

Let's test fixed overlay clipping. Implement media query and screenshot.

Also adapt title overlay for mobile: since `data-screen=title` CSS is now full-screen scrim + centered card; existing 760px media query handles panel + center. Good. With `position: fixed`, height 844, card ~500px tall, fits.

Also ensure overlay above topbar: overlay inside stage stacking context; topbar z-index 1; stage-wrap z-index 1. Stage is inside stage-wrap; stacking order: topbar and stage-wrap both z-index 1, DOM order stage-wrap after topbar → stage-wrap paints above topbar. Overlay within stage should paint above topbar (position fixed, z-index 6 inside stage). But topbar's help button at z-index auto within topbar stacking? Should be under overlay. Good.

Overlay backdrop-filter blur might blur the page behind including topbar? Fullscreen fixed overlay covers viewport.

Let's add CSS:
```css
@media (orientation: portrait) and (max-width: 720px) {
  .rotate-hint { display: inline-flex; }
  .underbar { display: none; }
